#passage(("In Islam, there are 5 types of commands. First one is complete Prohibition, which is what we call Haram. A lot of people be saying \"Haram, haram, haram!\", \"meme-ing\" that word, but this is the absolute prohibition, which means you cannot do this, you shouldn't do this, except under, for example, duress or under absolute necessity, where you have no other option. For example, you're going to die out of hunger, so you would have to eat pig, this is the only thing available, and you'd eat as much to SURVIVE, and you're not happy eating it or enjoying it, but you just want to survive. But in all other situations, that is absolutely prohibited. So, that is haram.", "Then we have something else that's called مكروه. Makruh is something that is the opposite of recommended, which means that if you leave it, God will reward you, but if you do it, He does not consider it a sin, He doesn't punish you for it. Leaving it is good, but doing it is not necessarily a problem.", "And then we have something in between, which is called مُباح (Mubah), which is something that is allowed. Islam doesn't say do or do not do. For example, scratching your head, you want to move your glasses, these types of basic things. That's the grey area in between, if you want to call it like that.", "And then we have مُستحَب (Mustahab), which is the opposite of Makruh, which is something that is recommended. If you do it, you get rewarded. If I help that individual, that is a recommended act that I will get rewarded for. But at the same time, if I don't do it, I'm not going to get a sin, unless that person is going to die and you can save his life; that's a different situation.", "And then, the last thing is the things that you have to do, فَرْض (Fardh), that you must do, like praying 5 times a day, etc., and paying your Zakat, which is the obligatory wealth-charity that you have to give yearly on the amount of savings that you have, if you're a rich person, which eradicates this whole idea and scenario, if people followed anyways. Eradicates the existence of those people who are poor, who don't have anything. Because either way, whether you give it recommended or not, you would have to give charity either way. If you have money, if you have savings, you have to, by obligation, give 2.5% of your wealth yearly, the savings that you have, if they surpass a certain amount. And, generally, giving charity is something that is recommended in Islam and commanded in the Quran and the Sunnah. And helping people, the more the person is in need, the more reward you would get from an Islamic perspective.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Attempts To Challenge Muslim Then It Backfires! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hA_fqvgV8sU", ((497, "8:17–9:03"), (549, "9:09–9:21"), (566, "9:26–9:39"), (582, "9:42–10:03"), (608, "10:08–10:55"),))
// U ML069:48-79 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Whether it's atTabari, alQurtubi or Ibn Khathir or any of these classical exegesis scholars, all of them are not authority for us as Muslims. For us as Muslims, what is authority is the following: The Quran (verbatim Word of God), the text of the Quran itself, clear text; and The Hadith (Tradition) of the Prophetﷺ; then after that we have something called إجماع (Ijma'), which is a Consensus of Muslims, Muslim Scholars, at a specific age, upon a specific issue; and after that you have قِياس (Qiyyas), which is like a form of analogy. So these are the sources of Revelation for us. These are things which someone can bring and say, OK, here is this!", "Now scholars in Islamic history, every single scholar you can name, we can take and reject from them. So in Islam, we have four schools of thought; you may have heard about Shafi'i, Maliki, Hanafi. Practically, all of the heads of these schools of thought said the same thing. They said: if my statement goes against the Quran or Hadith or Ijma', for instance, then throw it away. Imam Malik said: \"every person you take from them and reject\" (every scholar, including himself) \"except the person in this grave\", and he pointed at the grave of Prophet Muhammad. The only one we take everything from, when it comes to religion, is Prophet Muhammad, because he's a Messenger of God, and he's speaking Revelation. So Ibn Khathir can make mistakes, atTabari can make mistakes, and they did make mistakes in their Tafasir (exegeses).",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Questions Muslim On Young Marriages Then Instantly Regrets It! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zHZPZujQbd8", ((205, "3:25–4:03"), (246, "4:06–4:53"),))
// U ML221:339-370 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Allah gave us standards, principles that we can use to extrapolate rulings of anything new that will come. There are many principles; one of them is analogy: you make something analogous to the other. And there are general principles. I already give you a general principle about harm: you [can't] harm anyone or yourself. Whatever is going to come in the future that would harm is therefore, by that principle, impermissible. Another principle: The Prophet said whatever intoxicate is alcohol, and alcohol is impermissible. So any intoxicate in the future that takes you out of your rational faculties, drugs, heroin, whatever you want to call it, that, 1,400 years ago, already includes it. So there are these foundational principles that we use to extrapolate rulings to do things that are coming in the future, and God is all knowing; he knows what it's going to come.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “University Students Surprise Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTEkpj70bQ0", ((643, "10:43–11:38"),))
// U ML064:1566-1618 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Now the idea of inflexibility is false. Because Islam, even though it claims to be an eternal tradition, as he says, at the same time Islam says that we have this idea: Allah says in The Quran (anNisa4:83) (If a news comes to them of safety or fear, that they panic) (But if they return it to the people of knowledge), people of استِنباط (Istinbat), people who can look and study the tradition and come up with rulings, come up with an understanding based on the teachings of The Quran and Sunna. So in Islam, even though the Islam is an eternal text, it has within it this inbuilt system of rules and regulations, which can apply to every day and time and space until the day of judgment. And that's why you can have rulings with anything new. Anything that comes about, any issue that happens, we can have rulings in Islam that account and explain and answer these things. All of the things that we have in the modern day and age, Islam has a ruling regarding it; it's based on the teachings of the Quran and Sunna. So it's not inflexible. It's actually the most flexible tradition, because it is meant to stay until the end of times.", "And to say that Islam is outside history, it makes no sense. Allah tells us in The Quran (arRum30:42) قُلْ سِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَٱنظُرُوا۟ (Walk in the earth and see the outcome of the people who were before you). Explicitly in The Quran, Allah tells us the history of the people, of the Children of Israel; Allah tells us the history of the different civilisations, the beliefs of the people of Abraham. So, Islam is within history, where it talks about the people in the past, what they did, the mistakes that they committed, so we don't commit these mistakes. And also The Prophet tells us about the future, the prophecies of the future, what's going to happen in the future, what to avoid, what are the main major issues to avoid. But Islam also teaches you to increase in knowledge, and that's why we had this flourishing age of Islamic civilisation, which you were talking about. That comes from the idea of Islam pushing this idea of being an innovative person and inventing new things when it comes to the worldly aspect.", "But with Islam, yes, there are certain things, you used the word \"dogma\". If you mean by dogma something that Muslims believe that they stay upon? Yes, there is something that remains to be the same, because the Truth doesn't change. Truth is absolute, it's unchanging. So in Islam, the beliefs when it comes to God has always been the same and always remain to be the same. But the worldly aspects like science, technology, these things can change. And the basic rulings in the day-to-day and what to do in the dealings between the people, these also can change in time and place. But the core beliefs when it comes to who God is and what God does and what Revelations He sent down, these are absolute facts and truths that don't change, and these are the aspects of Islam that needs to remain the same. And that is a point in the favor of Islam, because if your core beliefs change, you're never going to be united, and you're going to have these conflicting ideas which keep changing, with people who fight with each other, and what the religion once was in the future is going to become something completely different, because there is nothing that is a foundation of the Faith that people can come back to.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((5140, "1:25:40–1:26:35"), (5201, "1:26:41–1:27:30"), (5255, "1:27:35–1:28:28"),))
