#passage(("But this is the thing: let's say, okay, you don't like this, \"barbaric, evil\", \"I'm upset, I don't like it\". Okay, what alternatives are you offering us? You're offering us: okay, let's take your money, the taxpayers money, and build many, many buildings, and buy a lot of food, buy a lot of equipment, buy a lot of cells, and spend all your money on that, and then get these people and separate them from their families completely, put them in these places, the people who committed the crimes, keep them in this place, psychologically and physically torturing them for long period of time, letting them network with each other, and communicating with another gangster outside: \"Oh, I'm coming out in 2 weeks, I'll see you there\", and get this lawyer who knows how to play around and run away from the crime completely, comes out more educated, more networking, bigger Network, more educated on new ways of stealing, taking taxpayer money, taking him off from his family.", "That's why you see this one guy who one time was arrested; he said, \"2 years, I'll be out. Okay, you captured now, so what? Two years.\" So how is that a solution, is the question. How does that solve the situation? Or when you do this to someone, [amputate] someone's arm: it's even very difficult for someone to do something like this again, and if he does, the other one will go. And someone seeing that is thinking 10 times, 100 times before: \"Oh, you know what, if I take something, this could disappear.\" So this is way more of a deterrent than to think you're going to go and meet your friend Michael, who's been there already for 5 years, and network with him a little bit, chitchat, and then go out and do it again. So we were saying, well, have you offered better alternative? We already explained the [Sharia's] perspective regarding this, but where is the alternative that you're offering anyways, as a liberal government or society? Humans are not the ones who are set to make these laws; it's always deficient.", "And that's why you see here in New York: I was in New York with a group of friends, and the guy had his laptop, and he said, \"No, no, take it from the back seat, because they would break in and take it.\" I'm like, is this supposed to be New York? You're worried to leave your [laptop]; if someone sees it from the [window], you're going to break it and take it. This is the alternative that you're offering us. And you see how many of these non Muslims speak about Dubai and talk about Muslim countries: whereever they go, they leave their phone. There was this football player as well, I don't know his name, he said he lost his wallet; eight months later or something, [he gets] someone calling him and looking for him, giving him the same amount of money, every penny he had inside. Subhan Allah. So you see, they say leave their stuff for 3, 4, 5 years and they come back. You leave your phone for 3, 4, 5 days, you come back, it's still there, because there is a punishment and laws and deterrence and things applied in the way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Eddie @TheDeenShowTV Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Masjid Dar Al-Qur'an | Chicago - USA”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sLfJyJGj_eg", ((2938, "48:58–50:12"), (3017, "50:17–51:57"), (3117, "51:57–52:53"),))
