#passage(("Many countries today have a death sentence. Oh, you don't agree with the death sentence at all. Let's assume I did what the guy who was in Germany did (we don't even need to mention his name), and I killed 7 million Jews or something like that. You believe I should not have a death sentence? If I go to the park and then rape little children day and night, you believe I don't deserve to die?", "But it is in the hands of Allah. Look, I'm the ruler. If I tell you, go slap this guy in the face, still in my hands. You're just enforcing my judgment. We're saying that the ruling of stoning comes from Allah; we're just people who are enforcing that law which is already in place. So we agree with you: it should be to Allah. We're not the ones who determine that. It's objective morality that comes from Allah. Allah is The One who determined X should have punishment Z. And Allah said that laymen should not implement it. It has to be an Islamic Society, and the government has to apply it.", "Do you agree with capital punishment for certain crimes or not? Even on a liberal point of view, you would find the fathers of liberalism saying that they agree with the capital punishment, like John Stuart Mill and John Locke. If you read their writings, they say that it does make sense and it is needed as a deterrent in society for certain crimes like treason. And it exists in America and many other countries. So, there are these sentences for certain crimes. It all depends on the gravity of the crime, and who is determining the gravity of the crime is high or low.", "I'm saying it is objective from an Islamic point of view. But even if you look at it from a liberal point of view, it becomes subjective, and then there is measurement and who is measuring it. This is subjectivity. In one land, we say this is the gravest crime from a liberal point of view. In another land, they say it's not. For example, they were putting the homosexuals to death before the 1960s. Now, if you just