// U ML183:613-698 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("At the time of the Prophetﷺ, there were people who were hypocrites in Medina, and they were not true believers, but they were outwardly showing they're believers; in their heart they were not believers. Allah told the prophet the names of these people, and his Companion Hudhayfa had a list of the hypocrites with their names. But the prophet did not punish them! Why? Because they did not commit apostasy. And you're only punished for what you do outwardly, what we see you doing. We cannot punish you for what you have in your heart. So if someone leaves Islam and he keeps to himself and he never says it, that person will never be punished in an Islamic state. Not only that they were disbelievers, but the Prophet even knew, and he didn't punish them, because they didn't outwardly, publicly say that they were. And they did not create division.", "Because the first thing someone who leaves Islam will do, he will have an issue with the laws. Because if you're living in a Sharia state now, which is the only state that the punishment would be applied in, you're not going to be happy with the laws there about not drinking, not gambling publicly, not fornicating. You'll say: \"I'm an apostate, I don't believe in them, I want to do whatever I want.\" So you will try to cause corruption in the land, and you'll try to make a group of people that cause corruption. That's naturally what will happen. You'll try to entice other people to also do the same.", "Many scholars like Sheikh alIslam Ibn Taymiyya and others mentioned that, if he's sincere and we can see that his intentions are there, that period can take a very, very long time, for them to try to talk to him and clear up his doubts until he comes back to the Truth. And I know someone who is in Libya, for example. He was someone who left Islam, and the government arrested him for that, because he publicly said that. And then now it's been six or seven years, and they're still bringing him people till this day, answering his doubts. And most of his doubts are answered. Not everything, but they're still bringing him people until this day. And we're talking about today.", "So the only person that then would be punished is after giving him a period of repentance, bringing him Scholars, telling him everything; they will say to him: \"it's either you repent, come back to Islam, or you'll be punished.\" Now, he can be a person who's arrogant, who doesn't want Islam, even though the evidences are shown. Then he has a way out. He says: \"OK, I come back to Islam\", and then he goes lives his life as a disbeliever, but not publicly declaring that he's a disbeliever, and he will not be punished. Of course, he will be punished in Hellfire for eternity, when he dies. But in this life that law is not going to be applied to him.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tyson Hockley Grills The Muslim Lantern With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XGBP8LOEhg8", ((1615, "26:55–27:41"), (1662, "27:42–28:06"), (1744, "29:04–29:37"), (1798, "29:58–30:26"),))
// U ML194:156-208 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("You said, \"Do you want people to be Munafiqoon?\" The response to that question is: no, I don't want people to be Munafiqoon. I want people to accept the Truth. That's the only thing I want. Now if someone lives in a Muslim state and he doesn't accept Islam, he's always welcome to leave! There are rules in that Muslim state, Government rules, Sharia rules; one of them is to do with Apostasy. I don't want him to be a Munafiq: let him leave. Go somewhere else. You don't have to be here if you have issues with the Islamic rules in an Islamic state. You don't have to live in that Islamic state; you can go! That's what you'll see at the time of the Prophetﷺ when he had the Sulh alHudaybiya (Treaty), for example. You had people who apostated; the Prophet sent them back to Quraysh. It's not about them being Munafiqoon: they apostated, and he let them go back to Quraysh. He said: \"You want to go back? Then go back.\" That was one of the rules of the Treaty of alHudaybiya, and there are Scholars who say based on that we can do the same thing. If someone wants to leave, they can leave; they can pretend and say \"I'm Muslim\" and leave. Nobody's forcing you to stay.", "If someone doesn't want to believe, then they leave without breaking the rules and the laws in that Society. One of these laws is that you cannot go around preaching disbelief to people, that you left Islam, \"I'm no longer a Muslim.\" You're welcome to live! Live your life, do whatever you want to do, but don't go around to the people saying: \"I left Islam, leave Islam, Islam is wrong\" and all of this stuff, like many people do today.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Dramatic Apostate Confronts Muslim On Islamic Law! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8G6UoAmYjYU", ((402, "6:42–7:40"), (476, "7:56–8:15"),))

