#passage(("If we follow your understanding, we will fall into contradiction. If I say (La Ikraha) is General: \"La\" means \"No\". \"Ikraha\" means \"Force.\" So, there is no forcing in \"Deen.\" There are words in the Arabic language that can have multiple meanings, depending on the context. To bring it in the context of Surah Yusuf does not mean it's the same meaning in Surat alBaqarah. But let's accept, no problem, it's talking about law. So, \"There is no compulsion in the laws\": the application of the laws essentially, in the laws of Islam.", "The Quran is full of punishments, meaning, it's mentioning verses about punishment. For example, lashing. That lashing is going to be forced. You're not going to ask \"Did you want to be lashed, when you committed or did this crime?\" That's not going to be there! That punishment will be applied whether they like it or not. So \"ikrah\" is going to happen when we take your flawed understanding. If we were to take the incorrect understanding, we fall into contradiction, and that's why we understand that this is not talking generally about everything in religion. It's not talking about after the acceptance. It's referring to the acceptance of the religion itself: if someone was not accepting the religion of Islam before, then you cannot force them to accept Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Dramatic Apostate Confronts Muslim On Islamic Law! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8G6UoAmYjYU", ((1018, "16:58–18:17"), (1138, "18:58–19:37"),))
