#passage(("First, the idea of slavery. There's no such thing as slavery in Islam. The Prophetﷺ said explicitly in a hadith: \"Do not say عبدي ('abdi: my [male] slave) or أمتي (amati: my [female] slave).\" You cannot say \"my slave\". So he prohibited us from using that term. Because slavery, in the sense of the word that everyone understands: if you go and Google slavery, you'll see a definition saying that you own a human being. You have a [possession], an owning of that person. And then you have forced labour on that individual. You force that person to do labour. You lash them. They have no dignity. That does not exist in the religion of Islam AT ALL. So you take someone who's free, you kidnap them, and then you enslave them. That's what slavery is. They become your property. You have forced labour on them. You hit them. That is the definition of what everyone understands of the word slavery, and that does not exist in Islam.", "What exists in Islam is something called مِلكُ اليَمين (Milku alYameen), and they are captives of war. Milku alYameen, or right-hand possession, are specifically to do with being captives of war. In fact, the Prophetﷺ told this: \"There are three people that Allah would be enemy of, on the Day of Judgement.\" One of them is he takes that free person, and then he makes him a slave! That's PROHIBITED in Islam.", "So, when Islam came, slavery already existed. It existed in the Roman Empire. It existed throughout history. Islam did not introduce slavery. Slavery was a part of society. Islam regulated slavery, and removed that term slavery, and introduced something completely different, which is the concept of Milku alYameen. It can only be open through WAR. Now, you have a war with an army. Two armies are fighting, the Muslim army and the non-Muslim army. Now, not everyone that you take and you capture becomes a Milku alYameen. Actually, the leader of the Muslim army decides. So, Islam restricted it in two ways. In the first way, only kept it