#passage(("Let's say a hypothetical situation: something is going to fall and it's gonna either hit a baby or an animal, whichever animal you want to think about, cat, dog, any animals. Let's say it's the same distance and you got to make the choice between either saving a human baby or an animal, whichever animal it is. Which one are you going to choose? You can only pick one. What does your innate disposition tell you?", "The difference is, yes, there are bad evil human beings that don't care about the suffering of people or animals, but we're talking about a baby. A baby is an infant, a small suckling child that doesn't have the cognition and the ability to make evil decisions about other people in the world. So, he's super innocent, didn't do anything wrong. I cannot apply the analogy: because there is an evil adult, therefore I'm not going to save a baby. Baby always takes a priority.", "And this is why I find an issue. My number one issue with the vegan community — not everyone is the same — or the vegetarian community: I meet many people who are vegan or vegetarian and, even if they don't admit it, from their behavior it seems that they put animals before human beings, because in reality there's a lot more important issues, I would claim, for us to talk about. For instance, what's happening in Palestine, Gaza, as an example. You have tens of thousands of children, babies are being killed. I think that's a more important issue, and it's not just Palestine. Same is happening in Sudan, same thing is happening all over the world. I think that's a more important issue for me to talk about and bring awareness about. If I'm seeing a child that is losing his limbs, is being bombed into pieces, that for me is more important than I talk about an animal.", "And I'm not saying that the animal doesn't matter. I'm not saying, okay, therefore we can justify the suffering that happens to animals. No, but we need to have priorities as human beings. When it comes to value, that's why I asked you a question: who holds higher value? We as human beings, we are actually able to hold conversation. We have an intellect that is God-given to us. We're able to build civilizations. By definition, we are in a higher level than these animals are. The fact that we're able to subdue and submit these animals is by itself proof that we are superior to them in a way. Even if someone says no, but that's the reality. If I'm able to subdue you and submit you, and I have higher cognition than you, I have ability to build civilization, I have ability to do all of that, by definition I'm more superior to you in a way.", "So, if you know that there is a creator, the question I want to ask is this: why do you think the creator created animals? The animals must have a purpose. Why? Because God created everything for a reason. God is all knowing, all powerful. You're a believer in God. So, you know that everything in the universe serves a purpose. God doesn't do things out of play and game. He's not like us. So if he creates something, there is a purpose behind it. Now I can make a claim God did it for that reason, God did it for this reason, but the only way you can know for a fact is for God himself to tell you why he created them. So Allah tells us in the Quran that he created animals for our sustenance. That doesn't mean that we abuse them. There's a balance. A lot of people take two extremes: either absolutely abuse animals, or completely leave animals in existence.", "There is a way of me killing animals for the fun of it, for example, like people who go hunting, playing for the sake of doing it, and they start shooting animals and killing them. And on the other hand, someone who is actually going to use, for instance, the fur of the animal for clothing, or is going to eat the animal for sustenance, for food. It's not the same thing. I'm not making the claim that we do it because we need it. I'm also not making the claim there's no enjoyment at all, but I'm making a claim in the middle. I'm saying the [Creator] created them for our sustenance. This is one of the blessings that god bestowed upon us. So of course there's a form of benefit and enjoyment that you get from it.", "I wouldn't call that abuse if it is within the [remit] of what God has allowed me to do. What defines abuse is something that God tells me is abuse. That's why I give an example: prophet, for example, forbid us from even playing with animals. You cannot play with them for fun, anything that has a soul. You cannot torture anything with fire. You cannot do that for the sake of fun and enjoyment. But for eating, which you can undoubtedly have benefits, you can say, okay, we can have alternatives, like, for example, take vitamin B or whatever: if you don't have it in meat, you can take it from pharmaceuticals or something like that. At the same time, you can take it from the meat.", "I'm not saying that when Allah says he created them for our sustenance, that means that only when we need it. No, it's general sustenance, and that can include, like I'm telling you, clothes; it's not just the food that you eat, can include fur and clothes and all of these different things. Allah doesn't say in the Quran you have to do this or you don't have to do that. But Allah gives you the remit where you're able to do that. It's morally okay for you to do the action.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Vegan Activist Questions A Muslim On Animal Cruelty In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eR81CSQZ6GA", ((135, "2:15–2:55"), (219, "3:39–4:03"), (243, "4:03–5:01"), (301, "5:01–5:49"), (363, "6:03–7:27"), (461, "7:41–8:53"), (542, "9:02–9:38"), (605, "10:05–10:26"),))
