#passage(("You're harming bacteria. You're killing it every moment right now. By breathing, you're killing bacteria. I was just talking about what you said, that you're not harming anything at all. That's why I was saying it's impossible. If you live in this life, if you just exist, by the virtue of you existing, there's some harm taking place. Even using any products, buying anything, doing anything is causing harm to someone somewhere in the world.", "I don't believe it's morally wrong for me to eat an animal. Where do you get the morally right or morally wrong from? Under an Islamic paradigm, it's not morally wrong to eat an animal, because the creator created the animal. So the question is, why is that specific thing wrong? You cannot just say because, for example, you're taking a life of an animal. Okay, why is that? Who determines if that is right or wrong? If the creator is the one who gave life to the animal, then it would also make sense that the creator allows you to take the life of the animal in the way the creator prescribes it. That's the argument I'm making. If the creator is the one who created me and you, and he created the animal, and he said, \"I create the animal and I allow you within these parameters to eat an animal,\" then that's okay. That's morally right.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Vegan Activist Questions A Muslim On Animal Cruelty In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eR81CSQZ6GA", ((786, "13:06–13:38"), (833, "13:53–15:04"),))
