#passage(("The difference is that the creator is the one who determines what you can and cannot do, what you can and cannot eat. So, in Islam, we have specific groups of animals that you're not allowed to consume or eat. The prophet, for example, tells us: any animals that have — I don't know how to say the word in English — nab, these very long teeth in your mouth, like tigers and whatever, these animals, any derivatives of these animals, you cannot eat. And, for instance, any animals with strong [claws], let's say hawks, and all of these animals that are predators that eat other animals, you're not allowed to eat. So we have certain parameters that the prophet has given us: you can't eat this, you cannot eat this. There's other animals that are also specifically mentioned. So that's why I'm telling you I have a morality that tells me exactly what is allowed, what is not allowed.", "And also those animals that you can eat, the slaughtering of these animals has to be done within a very specific way. In Islam, for instance, prophet forbade us from slaughtering an animal where another animal is watching, because that's already a form of mental suffering and is bad for the other animal. So it's not only suffering when the animal sees the other animal, but also hormones are released because of fear when the animal sees the other animal. So that's why I'm saying it's not necessary for you to show an animal another animal being slaughtered. The other animal that I'm slaughtering, I'm actually slaughtering for eating. I'm not slaughtering it for enjoyment. On the other hand, if the other animal sees this animal, then I'm making it suffer for no purpose, no reason. So the parameters is what the creator commands you that you're able to do, you're not able to do.", "Now you are, for example, telling me this is okay, this is not okay, but it's just your opinion; you said you get it from your heart. I have my heart and I can say, okay, I want to do it for the sake of doing it. What are you going to say? If morality is just subjective, is people's opinions, anyone can come up with the opinion. You heard that person who's telling you it's okay that he wants to abuse animals, he doesn't care. If someone tells you that, where are you going to get the objective morality to say no, you're wrong, and what I'm saying is right? That's why I say if you just say my heart, he can also say my heart.", "If I cut your throat, if I put you in the gas, it's very different. A lot of people also in the vegan and vegetarian community don't understand the purposes behind the rulings in Islam. So, in Islam, there's a very specific way to slaughter. Cutting the throat here is a very specific point. Why? Even the prophet tells us to sharpen the blade. Now you ask, okay, why? That makes no sense to me. But there are very important reasons behind this. I'll give you an example. Let's say you're cutting fruit or vegetables. I have a very sharp knife. You know what happens when you cut your finger? You don't notice it. You don't feel it, because the knife is so sharp, you don't even feel that point where it goes through your skin. Then later on, you realize, oh, I actually cut my finger. So the sharper the knife is, the less the pain you feel. Sometimes you don't even feel pain.", "When you cut here within the neck of the animal, you cut the blood connection that goes to the brain. So you don't feel pain anymore. Just a few seconds. Now if you put an animal in a gas chamber, it continues to suffer, because the pain receptors are there. The blood is going from the heart to the brain. It's not the same thing at all whatsoever. So when Islam gives these commands: a few seconds of pain, and then you cut this kind of connection between the pain receptors that the animals have, and the knife is very sharp. Therefore, they don't feel the pain. So it's a matter of few seconds.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Vegan Activist Questions A Muslim On Animal Cruelty In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eR81CSQZ6GA", ((916, "15:16–16:06"), (966, "16:06–17:19"), (1039, "17:19–17:59"), (1107, "18:27–19:11"), (1151, "19:11–19:44"),))
