#passage(("Do you think I behave based on what I determine is good and bad? I live my life as a Muslim based on the one who knows everything, who determines for me what I can and I cannot do. I can eat certain things; as long as they're permissible, then that means I'm allowed to eat them. [Excess] in eating them, that's a different story. So, for example, sugars: someone can eat sugars; excessing in it is a problem. Someone can eat fast food once a [week], is not a problem, but excessing in the thing is a problem. Allah says eat and drink, do not excess. So the limit is the excess. You can eat, you can drink, you can wear, you can do all of these things. Islam is not about being a monk, bald, not married.", "Would anyone disagree that eating fast food everyday is excess? No. There are objective standards that all human beings are going to agree this is excess. So there are objective [standards]; it's not all subjective, as you said. Second thing is, what determines excess or not is the damage that will do to you, because you're not allowed to harm yourself. Allah says do not throw yourself into damnation. These are the objective standards, because it's damaging your health. I'll guarantee you, whatever question you bring, Islam has an answer for it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “University Students Surprise Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTEkpj70bQ0", ((439, "7:19–8:08"), (506, "8:26–9:27"),))
