#passage(("The Prophet Muhammadﷺ has given descriptions of social behaviors, what we are supposed to do as human beings, what we should and we shouldn't. We need to add, how many more PhDs now? We need to add PhDs in finances, PhDs in people management. Keep adding PhDs! \"But he's a Prophet of God?\" \"No, no, he was the most intelligent, knowledgeable person upon existence, but was he a Prophet of God? No!\" \"But he said he's a Prophet of God.\" \"No, I'm not going to accept that.\" And as we said, some people, no matter how many evidences you show, they're not going to accept the Truth.", "So you have certain teachings, and you compare societies of today and look at the teachings of Islam. Some people will say these are simple teachings. You look at finances for example in the Quran, with the laws to do with interest and usury ربا (Riba), and the wisdom behind it. There's a beautiful video I recommend people to watch. It's called \"The Wisdom Behind the Prohibition of Riba\" by our brother Almir Colan. He goes into the details; it's like a 60 minute video. He talks about how interest and usury, if it's applied as a concept on a global scale, and it was applied in the past, how it destroys the community and the society. How these few Ahadith (Prophetic sayings) of the Prophetﷺ, to do with how to engage in dealing and trading, the gold only with gold, hand with hand, and all of these laws in the Quran and the Hadith to do with dealings and finances, how it saves a complete society. It's not just the Prophetﷺ saying do this and that, but it completely saves the society, the community, completely makes it function the correct way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Islam Unveiled: Compelling Evidence For The Truthfulness Of Islam | Chicago - USA | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AUFsBco_CF0", ((3132, "52:12–52:44"), (3168, "52:48–53:46"),))
// U ML315:83-147 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("Usury and interest are Haram. And the Prophetﷺ told us something very important. He told us 1400 years ago that \"usury and interest will widespread so much that even if believers Muslims try to avoid it the dust of interest will hit you\". Meaning that you will have certain effects: for example, even though I don't deal with interest and usury, I have to use a bank, and the bank deals with the interest and usury. But that doesn't mean that now I'm going to use a credit card, because I can use a debit card, so I'm not forced to use it. So the things which are necessities, you've got no choice except to deal with them; in Islam, these are exceptions. The things that are luxuries, we don't have to do them, then we leave them for the sake of Allahﷻ.", "Dealing with real estate or doing business is not Haram. Doing business is permissible. No one is saying don't do business, don't deal with real estate; there are many ways that you can do it which is permissible. You can buy property cash fully and then you can refurbish a property, you can sell it. You can buy a property and leave it until the price increases, you can sell it. There are multiple different things that you can do regarding real estate that are permissible and Halal. But charging usury and interest is not! Because it is effectively using the weakness of someone: someone who doesn't have money, and you're using the fact that he's weak to get more money from him! And in fact usury and interest completely shatters and destroys societies. There's an excellent video that I recommend that you watch, \"The wisdom behind the prohibition of Riba\", from a brother who works in finances, and this is what he studied.", "So when Allah says something is Haram, there is always a reason why Allah says it is Haram. There is a wisdom behind it. And there is general rule that whenever Allah says something is Haram there is an alternative that you can do. You mentioned alcohol, you mentioned gambling, you mentioned all of these different things. There is always an alternative of something that you can do, that is permissible, that Allah has allowed you to do. Allah said don't eat pork; He's allowed you to eat chicken, allowed you to eat lamb, to eat whatever other meat that is there, fish; all the meat is permissible except, Allah is saying, don't eat pork! Allah is saying drink juice, drink water, drink honey, drink milk, drink everything, but don't drink alcohol.", "But because I understand you're coming from a background in which you're doing a lot of these things, you start thinking that everything is Haram. Because this was your past life, you used to do most of these things, and now these things have become prohibited, then you start thinking that everything is Haram. And the interesting that Allah says in the Quran: He mentioned the things which are Haram in chapter 5 of the Quran verse 2, and then the next verse says (they ask you what is permissible?) what is Halal? Then Allah says: قُلْ أُحِلَّ لَكُمُ الطَّيِّبَاتُ (Everything good is Halal). So everything good is Halal; anything that you will see the label Haram on, there is a wisdom behind it, because it is not good for you. Allah says in the Quran5:100 قُل لَّا يَسْتَوِي الْخَبِيثُ وَالطَّيِّبُ وَلَوْ أَعْجَبَكَ كَثْرَةُ الْخَبِيثِ (say that the good things and the bad things are not equal even if you like most of the bad things).", "You're saying for example that everything's Haram. Fun is Haram! Fun is not Haram, you can have fun. But I understand, because what you used to call fun and what you used to do, that was fun for you, is now Haram because it's not beneficial for you. Muslims have been living their life, they've not been doing these things, and they're having fun. We have friends, we play football, we go bowling, we do many things, you can go swimming; you can do things which are beneficial for you. And you can enjoy your time with your friends, you can play video games, you can do many things. It's not that Islam says fun is Haram, but Islam is saying these things which are bad are Haram.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Message To Brother Sneak∅ - Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qxPkIlR6sb4", ((240, "4:00–4:28"), (310, "5:10–6:11"), (379, "6:19–6:44"), (409, "6:49–7:21"), (450, "7:30–7:54"),))
