#passage(("Interest is forbidden in Islam. Of course, it's one of the Major Sins in Islam. But if you look, it's the only sin where Islam says you're waging war against Allah.", "In Islam, we have a rule called \"الضرورات تبيح المحظورات\": the things which are necessary makes the impermissible permissible. For instance, I'm gonna die right now. Pork is haram. I'm gonna die, and there is nothing to eat except pork. In order for me to live, to continue my life, I have to eat some pork. Islamically, you are allowed to eat the pork to survive, not because you want to eat it or you like it, but for the sake of survival, because it's a necessary thing. Similarly, alcohol: if there's alcohol, I'm gonna die from thirst. Then I can drink alcohol just to maintain my life. And as the limit for necessity, only eat as much as necessary. You don't eat even the whole pork. No! Eat as much as keeps you alive.", "So dealing with interest today became a necessity in Islam. Here, you cannot work without dealing with a bank which has interest and usury in its system. So Muslims are forced to deal with banks that run with usury and interest, but we deal with the banks that don't give us interest in return. I told them I want an account that doesn't give interest, a current account. I don't take anything from them; I'm just using the bank because I necessarily have to use the bank, to do work in this country. We don't mortgage. We don't take loans for university. That's impermissible. Some Muslims do it. Some Muslims disobey, make sins, like everyone makes sins, but islamically, you're not allowed to do these things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Christian Testimony About Islam Part 1! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qLF3sGWkBek", ((992, "16:32–16:39"), (1119, "18:39–19:25"), (1171, "19:31–20:37"),))
