#passage(("I understand that you perhaps have a struggle with music, but if you do have a struggle with music, that does not mean now that you can take that and then go with a general statement that there's nothing allowed that has creativity in Islam. That's completely incorrect. People can be very creative, whether that be in fields of medicine, for instance, whether that be in fields of technology. I was talking about the inventions of the modern Scientific Method. You have 'Abbas ibn Firnas, the first person to make a human plane. It's the first person to fly. So inventions in Islam has been always a part of human history. You have the inventions to do with algorithms. You have the inventions to do with medicine. You have inventions to do with many things.", "Even, for example, drawing, you're able to draw. You don't draw things which have a soul, but you're allowed to draw many other things. You can draw a lot of other things. You can be very creative with doing that. That's not an issue. You can become creative in programming. You can programme different things, come up with different algorithms, things which actually help society and helps people. You can be very creative in handmade things. And a lot of women do that. A lot of women love doing that. You see this, for example, lantern over here. They have someone who's creative who made it. There's nothing haram about that. There are no issues with any of these things. There's many, many things that people can make and can create that has creativity. There are specific things that Allah did not allow for specific reasons that He, the Most-Wise, knows is not beneficial for us as humans. That doesn't mean that creativity is not allowed.", "If scholars talked about the internet, for example, or any of these specific things, the phone, it was not said it was haram in the beginning. The phone was a very small function. It was to call other people. That's it. We didn't have scholars saying it's haram. Certain people who might have said: \"avoid this or don't do that\" is because they saw the harms within that thing. So, for instance, they saw this phone now. When there are screens now, people are looking at nudity pictures. It's influencing the kids. People are using it for the wrong reasons. People are using it for things which are not allowed. So it's not that they said making the phone is haram. That's not the case. Technology and things which are to do with this life that make things easier: when cars were made, nobody said this is haram. When aeroplanes were made, nobody said this is haram. Scholars are not telling you these things are haram. If they serve a function that is beneficial for the human being, it's not a harmful function for human beings, we don't have people saying it's haram.", "If someone wants to use cotton or something and make shapes, or want to use mud and make whatever it is, or collect coins, anything which requires creativity or as a form of hobby: these are examples of things that Islam allows which require creativity.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Questions The Rationality Behind Islamic Laws! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l86scGUnVXI", ((70, "1:10–1:57"), (117, "1:57–2:58"), (299, "4:59–5:57"), (367, "6:07–6:20"),))
