#passage(("Photography is not haram in Islam. Taking pictures of people, taking pictures of objects: nobody said that it's haram. There are people, individuals who said, but they're incorrect. When I say nobody, I mean there is not a correct view. Why is it not the correct view? Because they based it on a narration, a hadith, where the Prophetﷺ (peace be upon him) said: \"The Mussawiroon (photographers) are cursed\". And the Prophet is not talking about cameras. The Prophet is talking about people who make shapes and objects and idols and these specific things that they used to make, or of human beings. Why? Because that hadith says that Allah will say to them on the Day of Judgement, أَحْيُوا مَا خَلَقْتُمْ (Give life to that which you created). So it has to be something that the human being has made, have created, that resembles the Creation, that Allah will tell him, give life to. So if a picture of yourself, Allah is not going to tell you give life to yourself when you exist. It's just a reflection, just like a mirror, that is done with the light. That's how we have a picture. So it is not haram having pictures in Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Questions The Rationality Behind Islamic Laws! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l86scGUnVXI", ((498, "8:18–9:15"),))

