#passage(("Eid Al Fitr: the word “فطر” means to \"break fast\". It is the holiday of breaking the period of fasting, the 30 days that you've done, thanking God for the blessings, that he allowed you to live, and all of that. And also Eid, it's a very important thing, because there's obligatory charity to be given in Eid Al Fitr: a specific amount of material things that you need to give to the poor; everyone has to give it. So there is Eid Al Fitr, \"because we breaking in the fast\", and Eid Al Adha means to sacrifice: “أضحية” is a sacrifice. So Adha Eid, it's a celebration of sacrifice, and that tradition traces all the way back to Abraham, if you're aware of the story of Abraham and his two sons, and God told him not to sacrifice his son, and then God sent him a lamb, a ram or a lamb, whatever you want to say, and then he sacrificed it. That tradition, we imitate that tradition in a way, as a form of gratitude to the Creator. And also we distribute the meat: a portion of the meat every Muslim has to distribute it; we feed the poor people. So these are the two significance between these holidays. And also it's linked to Hajj, because it comes after Hajj, which's pilgrimage. So, it's also finishing the period of pilgrimage; now there's a celebration of Eid.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Non-Muslim Audience Asks Muslim Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=imKsFyzx-Yo", ((2142, "35:42–37:01"),))
// U ML282:1-111 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("Santa Clause, which is actually, if you think about it, quite creepy, isn't it? An old man, a fat old man, comes from a chimney to touch your little kids. If an old man coming down the chimney, fat guy with a big belly, with a beard, coming down my chimney, and to touch my little kids! I don't know what he's doing with my little kids, and then taking some gifts I leave for them. How is that related in any way, shape or form to the idea of Christianity and believing in Jesus? But the thing is, it still remains: the idea that Christians in their psychology believe that this is a celebration; many of them believe that this celebration of the date of birth of Jesus. And therefore, now it is the problem for the Muslims. Which Muslims? The Muslims who are celebrating this holiday, quote unquote holiday, which is a pagan day, according -as we just said- to Christian learned men; not the public people who don't even know anything about anything, but the learned people or historians clearly have made that point clear.", "So when a Muslim celebrates -and celebration can be anything- celebration can be saying to them a term which they use: the term that they use, \"Merry Christmas\". Saying that term, in that time, to someone, in that context, is a form of celebration. And that's why they always tell you Eid Mubarak, they tell us also Ramadan Mubarak or Ramadan Kareem, whatever term they hear from Muslims, because what is it? They're congratulating you, celebrating with you the coming of the holiday for you. Or celebration can be putting a Christmas Tree, which, as we just said, even the Bible, don't talk about Islam, calls it an idol. You're actually putting an idol there. And this is a huge problem. These people who have this Tree, you need to understand where the origin that it comes from.", "A lot of Muslims say \"they're not celebrating it as a Christian thing\". Whether they celebrate as a Christian thing or not, you pick one of two: it's either a Christian thing or a pagan thing. Pick your poison, as we say. Which one do you want to pick as a Muslim? Either way it's Shirk. You cannot try to escape this idea by pretending, because a lot of Muslim try to: \"they don't actually believe, it's just a holiday\". No! The origin of it is either Pagan, or that they do have beliefs that this is a Christian thing and that they believe that this is the time where god has his son!", "If someone disbelieving, a disbeliever sitting here, and he curses Allah: would you congratulate him for cursing Allah? What would me congratulating him for cursing Allah be considered? Disbelief; it's Kufr, not Shirk. This person has just cursed Allah and I congratulated him for cursing my God; do you understand how problematic that is? Why am I giving this analogy? Because this is effectively what happens. Because Allahﷻ says in the authentic Hadith that شتَمَنِي ابنُ آدمَ, Son of Adam has cursed me, and then Allah explains that he ascribed to me a Son. What is the curse that he did? He ascribed to me a son. وأنا اللَّهُ الأحدُ الصَّمدُ: I'm The One Unique, Uniquely One; الصمد, The Independent. So when he says الأحدُ الصَّمدُ, because if you have a son, that son is also God. الصَّمدُ: The Self-Sufficient, The Independent. Everyone depends on Allah alone; you cannot be depending on Jesus and Allah, then you're not الصَّمدُ anymore, you're not the one that is Sufficient for the needs of all the Creation. So that's why Allah mentions الأحدُ الصَّمدُ.", "And Allah says شتَمني ابنُ آدمَ, so Allah is telling you to ascribe a Son to Allah is cursing Allah, because initially you are demeaning the status of Allah: rather than saying he's One, Absolute, Self-Sufficient, you've ascribed a partner to Allah. And then a Muslim goes and he congratulates the person for cursing Allah, because effectively that's what the holiday is about: it is the celebration of the birth of the Son of God, and you have a Muslim who congratulates another person. Muslims need to understand this is effectively what you're doing. Whether you understand or you don't understand, it's effectively what you're doing. Or you're celebrating the association and the people who worship other than Allah and give sacrifices for other than Allah, so they can have good agriculture and good رزق (Rizq: Sustenance). And either way this is a problem of what you're doing. And the fact that there are Christians or many Christians that believe that this is the date that the Son of God was born, this is enough. Even if the truth is it isn't, they still believe it, so you congratulating them for it, you're congratulating them for that belief.", "The Muslims, the problem they have is, Allahﷻ says in the Quran, He says to the Prophetﷺ (AlQalam68:9) وَدُّوا۟ لَوۡ تُدۡهِنُ فَیُدۡهِنُونَ (They wish): this is what they wish, the disbelieving people, لَوۡ تُدۡهِنُ (that you compromise with them and they'll compromise with you). And this is what Muslims are effectively doing. They say to me: but he said to me last year Eid Mubarak! How can I not say to him Merry this and Merry that? How can I not compromise? And we know Surat Alkafirun (109:1-6): the people came to the Prophetﷺ and they said: you worship our God or Gods for a period of time, we worship your God for the same. Same exact thing: compromise with us a little bit; give us our \"Merry Christmas\" a little bit and we give you the \"Eid Mubarak\" a little bit. And then the Prophetﷺ completely rejected that. Why? Because the minute you do that, you've entered into Disbelief. There's no options of compromising this and compromising that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Reason Some \"Muslims\" Celebrate Christmas! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ti1oJEBzsOs", ((0, "0:00–1:02"), (62, "1:02–1:59"), (125, "2:05–2:38"), (163, "2:43–3:49"), (234, "3:54–4:58"), (371, "6:11–7:02"),))
