#passage(("The tree: we wouldn't say that the tree thing is shirk, but we say it's imitating the disbelievers, which is a Major Sin. Because in Islam, we don't have a tree that we bring. And the crescent is not an Islamic symbol. The Ottoman Empire used it in certain things; they are the ones who started using it, in a way, maybe you could say, in contrast to the Christians using a cross. But the Prophet was not putting crescents on the mosques or any of that. That's not something which is found in the Quran or Hadith. It's not an Islamic thing, meaning it's not based on the text. So the crescent itself, it's not an Islamic thing, number one.", "Number two, to bring a tree specifically and give gifts and do that is imitation of the disbelievers. And not only that, it gives legitimacy to what they do. Because if we do it, then also it's OK that they're doing it. What's the problem? We're doing it as well. They do it their way. We do it our way. It's the same thing. They do it and we do it. It's like two football teams: they're a football team and we're a football team. No, but this is wrong and this is not wrong. So you should not be doing it because, number one, it gives the print on the children that this behavior is good and it's OK. And they will grow up with that behaviour or with that tradition of having a tree. And they will believe that it's OK because Christians would do it that way and Muslims would do it that way.", "At the time of the Ottoman Empire, they were doing all of these crescents and whatever it is. It was done historically by Muslims. But it's not something that is based on the Quran or Hadith.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Compares Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Igzqbqr2Pew", ((1445, "24:05–24:35"), (1481, "24:41–25:15"), (1525, "25:25–25:38"),))
// U ML060:71-120 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Wearing red: it's only prohibited, or let's say [makruh], if you're wearing the two-piece garments and it doesn't have any other colors on it. So if you have a logo that is a different color, you're already out of the equation there. If you have a logo that is black, you're out of the equation. If you're wearing a t-shirt that is red and something else is black, you're out of the equation. A lot of people say: why would your god be upset that you're wearing a color, or some lazy argument like that. But the reason is mentioned in the hadith: the prophet said the people of the book at that time, they would wear this type of clothing; to differentiate yourself from them. People like: \"oh God doesn't like the color, this is the color of blood or the color of this\"; all nonsense. The hadith itself mentions the reason. The people of the book would wear clothing that is like saffron dyed. So the prophet say don't.", "But it's not only that. The prophet said many things. Prophet said let your beard grow, for example, and trim your mustache, because the people of the book do the opposite: they would grow their mustaches and shave their beard. So he said differentiate yourself from them. For example, the prophet said pray in your shoes because the Jews and the Christians don't pray in their shoes. But the shoes has to be clean, of course; has to be completely clean. There's nothing in it.", "But there's many teachings which the prophet specifically commanded which differentiates Muslims from other religions. It's very important people understand we don't have this idea of compromise: we'll do the same thing as you guys are doing. No. Christianity is a tradition. Judaism is a tradition. We have our own traditions and you have your own traditions. Even fasting on the day, the prophet say, because the Jewish people fast that day, fast the day before and the day after, so you don't do like them. You do something different. There is so many traditions like this. So prophet Muhammad was clear that the Jewish people, this is something specific to them. We're not talking about things that we and them do, both of us do. That's fine. You're not imitating them. We all eat the same way, we all maybe dress the same clothing today. That's not imitating. But things which they only do, like for example wearing a cross: you cannot have a Muslim walking around wearing a cross. That's something specific for the people of the book, of course, and it has all of this symbolic value behind it, the idea of crucifixion and all of that, which we don't believe in.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sneako Questions The Muslim Lantern On Shia & Sunni Unity | The Iran War And More | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kdFNc6Jr6OQ", ((140, "2:20–3:30"), (210, "3:30–3:55"), (235, "3:55–4:57"),))
