#passage(("This happens a lot. Some non-Muslims come to us and they tell us happy Eid or whatever. They tell us stuff like this. So now they are compromising, because Islam is not the religion, and they're coming. They've given us congratulations for a religion that is not the religion. So they're compromising in their own religion, but they expect us to do the same. They're being nice, but they're being liberal. Their worldview is it's okay to say to anyone good, happy celebration. So that is their worldview. That is a religion. That is what they're following. But they expect Muslims to compromise when it is their holiday, by also in return giving them greetings and giving them congratulations. It's not a free happy Eid. No, it's a happy Eid with the expectation that you also follow my liberal way: when my holiday come, you as a Muslim come and tell me merry Christmas or whatever.", "So that is why we say as Muslims we stick to our faith. When it is your religion, I'm not going to tell you happy holiday in your religion. And I don't need you to come to me in my holiday and tell me happy holiday. It's fine. I'm more than content and enough and following my tradition. In Islam, you follow the truth. We follow the truth. They're all welcome to become Muslim and they're all welcome to celebrate the holiday that is from God, that is good for you. You're welcome to become a Muslim and become a part of that. But the holiday that you're doing is associating with God. It's worshiping a human being. So because of that, we're not going to compromise and tell you happy holiday when it comes to your holiday. And we will celebrate ours, because we know it's the truth.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Rational Thinker Asks A Muslim Thought-Provoking Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=myrg5IOTPBI", ((1415, "23:35–24:40"), (1480, "24:40–25:54"),))
// U ML285:324-425 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("All of this \"mumbo-jumbo\" that was going on is to reach this point: Mawlid, the point of celebrating the Birthday of the Prophetﷺ. You have to do all of this: misrepresenting statements, attributing things to people they didn't do, calling things which are instructions and buildings Bid'ah. You have to do all of this just to try to legitimise the action that you do, which is celebrating the Birthday of the Prophetﷺ. Isn't this enough evidence to know that this is something not from the religion? If it was from the religion, you'll easily find the Hadith, you'll easily find a statement from a Companion, a Companion doing it. You'll go to that straight away and you'll save yourself time. But when you have to go in a circle like this, bring things that are not related, make up statements and all of that, so you can just say \"Mawlid\", it is clear evidence that you've got no evidence. You're just making things up in order for you to push forward your agenda.", "And then, he says that which \"agrees with the rules of the religion\". The rules of the religion say that every Bid'aah is misguided. There cannot be a Bid'aah Hasana there, because the Prophetﷺ, what did he say? He said every Bid'ah is misguidance. That's it! If it's anything you innovate, then it's against the religion. The Prophetﷺ said, \"There is nothing that brings you closer to Allahﷻ except that I've told you, and there is nothing that brings you closer to Hellfire except that I forbade you from\". So, if you tell us: \"this Mawlid -or whatever innovation you want to bring- it brings you closer to Allahﷻ\", then this opposes the Sharia, the religion, the statement that you just said. And if it doesn't bring you closer to Allahﷻ, then you are just bringing something into Islam and going against the tradition of the Prophetﷺ, where he said that you cannot bring anything into Islam. So those people who do the Mawlid believe that this is out of love for the Prophetﷺ and this brings you closer to Allahﷻ, but the Prophetﷺ himself already told you: \"There's nothing that brings you closer to Allah except that I've already told you\". And he says: مَنْ أَحْدَثَ فِي أَمْرِنَا هَذَا مَا لَيْسَ مِنْهُ فَهُوَ رَدٌّ (Whoever innovates anything in this religion that is not a part of it, then it's rejected). These are not my statements. I don't need to go in a circle like you. I don't need to do any of that. I can just tell you \"The Prophetﷺ said..\". Islam is very simple. \"The Prophet ﷺ said..\", that's it. Follow what the Prophetﷺ said and don't waste your time anymore.", "The Mawlid of the Prophetﷺ: if anyone celebrates the birthday of the Prophetﷺ and they want to say that this action brings you closer to Allah, or they want to say that this is a good thing, then they need to explain to us why the Prophetﷺ did not command it. Why did the Prophetﷺ not do it? Why did the Prophetﷺ, the Sahaba of the Prophetﷺ, who, you would agree with me, they love the Prophetﷺ more than you, unless you say that you love the Prophet more than the Sahaba; then you've got your own problems. So, if you say that the Sahaba -as you agree, and the Tabi'in and the Atba' Attabi'in, because they were the best of generations- they already loved the Prophetﷺ more than me and you or anyone else loves the Prophetﷺ, that means that if this action was good, and they love the Prophet more than you and me, then they would have done it. But you don't have the Sahaba or the Tabi'in or Atba' Attabi'in, or the year after it, or the year after it, or the year after, until we came to the فاطميين (Fatimids), hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of years later, where a group of people decided to innovate this idea of celebrating the birthday of the Prophetﷺ.", "Some of them will tell you, \"No, the Prophetﷺ celebrated his birthday. He did, but you just don't know!\" OK, when did the Prophetﷺ celebrate his birthday? They will say to you that the Prophetﷺ fasted Monday and he said, \"This is the day I was born in.\" Let me tell you the trickery here, and the funny trickery about this idea. If I want to accept their claim, \"The Prophetﷺ fasted Monday because he was born Monday\", did the Prophetﷺ fast the date that he was born? This is what a birthday is. You celebrate ربيع الأول Rabi' al-Awwal. Rabi' al-Awwal can come on a Wednesday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday; it can come any day. It doesn't have to be a Monday, because it's a specific date. So, when you say that you celebrate a birthday, you're not celebrating Monday, are you? Why are you not celebrating Monday then? You should be celebrating Monday, because the Prophetﷺ fasted Monday. So when he says \"the day I was born in\", he's referring to Monday; he's not referring to the date, which is 12 Rabi' al-Awwal. He was not looking at the calendar when it is 12 Rabi' al-Awwal and celebrating that day. Let's say I'm born on a Friday. That Friday has a specific date in the calendar. If I celebrate the specific date, sometimes it's going to be Friday, sometimes it's going to be Saturday, sometimes it's going to be Sunday. This is a birthday. But to celebrate Friday itself as a day, then this is a different story now.", "So they misattribute things to the Prophetﷺ of Allah and they say he celebrated his birthday. That was not his birthday. That was Monday, a specific day. And the Prophetﷺ did not say \"just because I was born in it\"; he said he fasted for a specific reason. He said he fasted Monday because: \"this is a day I was born on, it is a day I'm going to die, and it is a day that I was.. بُعِثت فيه.. given Messenger-hood in\". Because all of these events happened on a Monday, so the Prophet decided to fast on a Monday. And Thursday, because the deeds are uplifted to Allah, so he wants his deeds, when they're lifted to Allah, that his deeds are lifted when he's fasting. So, he's saying, because of all of these events happening on a specific day, I'm fasting that day. He's not saying, \"Celebrate my birthday, celebrate 12 Rabi' al-Awwal\", which, by the way, there's no evidence that this is the time that he was born. You even have people who say, \"OK, you can celebrate the birthday\", and they themselves tell you that we do not know when he was born exactly. How are you going to celebrate his birthday when you don't even know when he was born? People will try to do anything. They want to play music and dance and celebrate and do all of this stuff. That's why they will play with the religion, try to make up things to say, \"We should celebrate it, it's a part of Islam\".",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Quran Scientific Claim Debunked? Bida'ah Is Good? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5z_gp4XDYzs", ((1019, "16:59–17:51"), (1075, "17:55–19:08"), (1157, "19:17–20:11"), (1215, "20:15–21:21"), (1286, "21:26–22:27"),))
// U ML227:348-394 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("When it comes to going and reciting the Quran, there's two issues there. The first issue is going in congregation on a grave and reciting: I'll bring 100 people, and let's go to this grave, in congregation, we recite together or whatever it is, for the dead person, with the intention of us benefiting the dead person. That is not found in the Quran or Hadith; therefore it's something that people innovated. Now, on the other hand, reciting the Quran in a way in which it's not innovated, meaning you don't bring people and recite the Quran in the grave: let's say I'm going to recite the Quran in a way which is not innovated. I'm just normally going to read the Quran, and after reading the Quran, I have the intention that I want Allah to give the reward for this recitation to the dead person.", "The first issue is the way you're going to do the recitation. If that way is not presented in Islamic texts, like congregation, going together and reciting this and that on the grave, next to the grave, then this way is innovated. Reciting the Quran itself in general, by yourself or whichever way you're going to recite the Quran, then having the intention: ”Oh Allah, give this reward to the dead person”: now, this is an issue of disagreement. This is not haram. We can't say this is necessarily haram. Some scholars will say that the reward will reach the dead person, and some people will say that the reward does not reach the dead person. There's two opinions. Some people will tell you the reward does not reach the dead person, therefore there's no point of doing that: you are just going to get the reward of recitation you did. And others will say it's possible for it to go. So it's an issue of disagreement, and both parties have their own evidence for that. So if someone were to recite the Quran in a way which is not an innovation, and he wished the reward to reach, then that's not necessarily a problem. But I'm of the view that it's not going to reach, due to the strength of the evidence.", "When you do certain things continuously in congregation, which has not been legislated in Islam, it's a problem, because this is what we refer to as innovation. It's things which are done in a specific set way: a set way or set time or set day or specific way that you repeatedly do it in, that is not presently found in the Quran or Hadith, and you believe that this is good, the action is good; this is what we refer to as innovation. So these people can recite and then wish the reward goes to that person. But do not do it in a way which is innovated.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Compares Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Igzqbqr2Pew", ((1260, "21:00–21:44"), (1311, "21:51–22:55"), (1387, "23:07–23:41"),))

