#passage(("From an Islamic perspective, there's a lot of things that I can explain to you from a logical perspective, and there are certain things that I cannot explain to you from a logical perspective. I can say to you that this is a teaching of God and I'm following it because it's a teaching of God. The reason I'm telling you that is because, if I want to explain everything to you intellectually, and give you an intellectual reasoning behind it, we would say as Muslims: in reality worshipping your mind, you're not worshipping God! Because you're doing it because it makes sense to you; you're not doing it because God commanded you to do it! And we understand that Allah has Infinite Wisdom, and we have limited wisdom. Just like I cannot see behind this, my eyes are limited, I can't hear what's going on in the other room over there, also my mind is limited. Unfortunately some human beings think today that we can know everything. No! There are certain things that we're limited, that we cannot know.", "So it is a teaching of Islam that you cannot shake a hand, not with a woman; with the opposite gender, whether you are a man or woman. You cannot shake hand with the opposite gender if that individual is someone you can marry. With a few exceptions, but it's generally about someone you can marry. So if it's my sister, my mother, my family members, I can shake hand, I can hug, there's no problem (obviously my wife). If it's an individual that I can get married to, Islam limits physical interaction between these two individuals. And trust me, touching hands, it's a very physical thing, it's a very psychological thing; there is attraction that comes from it. You might not notice it, but there is a lot of psychological things that come; there's a lot of studies on this. That's why in movies you'll see they're sitting in dinner and then the girl or the guy puts his hand on the other girl. Why? He's just touching your hand, but they know it's a kind of a sexual act in a way that brings you closer to the individual. So Allah prohibited that for the wisdom that He knows. I'm just giving you a few clues.", "But generally it's a teaching of Islam that we do not touch that which is not ours. And when something is precious you do not touch it! When you go to the jewelry store, did they allow you to touch the gold and silver and jewelry? But if you go to Tesco you're going to touch everything! It's OK, it's cheap. But going to the jewelry store, they will say to you: this is very precious, it's gold, silver, you cannot just touch it unless you're gonna buy it. Unless you're a serious buyer they're not going to allow you to see the jewelry! The more precious something is, the more that thing is protected, and in Islam our women are the most precious for us. So that's why I wouldn't touch someone that I do not have an actual intent on being with.", "The people who do, they're not really practicing the teachings of Islam there. Just like there's Muslims who don't pray, or there's Muslims who don't fast. But if you go to them and you say to them: Did the Prophet say this? They will have to say: yes, the Prophet said this. But I'll tell you the real reason why they do this. The real reason they do this is because of peer pressure, because a lot of people in certain Western places or Western companies maybe they see that as a form of disrespect, and the person sometimes is afraid for his job: \"I don't shake your hand, I'm gonna be fired!\" So sometimes that happens. Sometimes it's just peer pressure and being embarrassed of showing your identity, which is not something good, I would say.", "If you go to Japan, try to shake a hand with anyone: no one would shake your hand; they bow down to the individual, a little bow, not completely, just a little bow. They will not shake your hand; they will say to you: this is our culture, thank you, and they will bow down. This is their beliefs; people would respect that to be their culture. But I think sometimes, when it comes to the Muslims and their interactions, a lot of people have not come across different cultures, did not come across different beliefs, did not come across different people, so they are thinking: why did he not do that specific act? You might think he's being disrespectful in a way, shape or form, but no! It's just an action that God commands us to do. It's a part of our religion, what we believe.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Non-Muslim Audience Asks Muslim Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=imKsFyzx-Yo", ((1879, "31:19–32:05"), (1928, "32:08–33:05"), (1989, "33:09–33:47"), (2037, "33:57–34:40"), (2087, "34:47–35:24"),))
