#passage(("The moral practices that we do is based on the wisdom of the Creator that knows everything. Now, you're applying a modern standard of liberalism (you don't even know it, but you are), which is the idea of consent. You cannot even define consent. At what age does the person consent? What indicates that the statement is considered consent? When can we say this is consenting, when is not? If a girl has one drink, is that consent or not consent, if she has intercourse with you? I can go on and on and on and tell you how ridiculous laws are in the UK when it comes to consent, and that's why we have a lot of issues: women saying \"I've been abused, I didn't actually consent, I had a drink\", whatever. There is no clear definition in the law. I've done research and you're welcome to do your own.", "Now, if a patient is under a little bit of hallucination, can he give consent? Can someone else give consent on his behalf? Who gives consent on his behalf? Consent, you might think is a simple thing; it's not a simple thing. But again, this is a very modern principle.", "You're saying a baby, in the case in which you would say is not able to consent anyways, so you will do the best decisions on their behalf. I'll use the same standard: a baby cannot consent, do not feed it milk! Maybe it wants water. Who told you to feed it milk? So now it's not about the consent. You arguing the same argument as me, so it's no longer about consent, it's about what is beneficial for the child. And therefore we say that's why we do it. Why beneficial for the child? Because the Creator commanded it, and the one who made you knows about me more than you. Like if I make this phone, I know every little detail of how it [functions] more than you would.", "And circumcision, it has an effect on the level of desire the individual have long term, for the rest of their life, the level of sexual desire, because what it covers, the most sensitive part. So it has an influence on [lessening] the desire of the man as well. So there are wisdoms that we can talk about, but essentially, we know by necessity: if God commanded it, he is all wise. There has to be [wisdom]; we can reach some of them, maybe you can not reach some of them.", "Who said to you to be cleaner? We don't say that. To be cleaner could be another wisdom, because it's from God, then it's wise. Now we can try with [our limited] intellect to find what are the wisdoms. Maybe the one wisdom is cleaner; it's not even one wisdom, there could be many. It's cleaner, one wisdom; another wisdom is idea of desire that I mentioned. You can go through many, many, many wisdoms. It's not just one wisdom.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “University Students Surprise Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTEkpj70bQ0", ((1305, "21:45–22:36"), (1360, "22:40–22:57"), (1377, "22:57–23:45"), (1425, "23:45–24:17"), (1465, "24:25–24:48"),))
// U ML274:272-313 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("You don't have to do it. This is a sunnah, which means it's a recommended act (a Prophetic practice, but not obligatory). It's recommended from Allahﷻ, it's not necessary for you to do. And if you don't want to do it, you don't have to. But it's an act of Sunnah.", "But the thing is this: when you say \"what is the logical reason behind this?\", there is an assumption. The assumption is that there has to be a logical justification that fits what you think is logical for every command in Islam. That's an assumption, because otherwise that question would not make any sense. Because if Allahﷻ commanded it and there's no need for me to have a logical justification, I'm not going to be asking for one! But there's an assumption, for many people, that there has to be a logical explanation given to me that fits my understanding for every command, in order for me to do that command. Then you're not worshipping God, you're worshipping your own desires, worshipping your intellect! So if it makes sense to my intellect, I'm going to do it; if it does not make sense to my intellect, I'm not going to do it.", "And the fact is, you can do this with any command in Islam. You can have a never-ending process. For example, why is it five prayers? And if it was six, you will say \"why is it six prayers?\" If it's 10, another person will come and say \"why is it 10 prayers?\" There are two types of commands in Islam: things which are تَعبُّدي, means we do as an act of worship. For example, the one you mentioned about shaving the head, this is an act of worship. And then there is other things that Allahﷻ might tell you the reason and the wisdom behind them.", "But the important thing what you need to establish with logical reasoning is whether Islam is the Truth or not. Once you've established that with logical reasoning and evidences, then you cannot be questioning every single thing, because you already now accepted this is literally God's command. Once I establish this is from God, then I know God is speaking there. If God is speaking there, I'm not going to be asking God every single thing He tells me to do: why should I do it and I shouldn't do it! I need first evidence that Islam is the Truth. OK, I've got evidence Islam is the Truth, I believe it's from God. Then I would follow what God commands! Because He's God! He's the All-Knowing, the All-Wise, He is the Creator, He's The One Who created me. And I'm in this life to obey and worship the Creator.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “American Lady Questions Muslim About Women In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wloKwQx2KcA", ((997, "16:37–16:47"), (1015, "16:55–17:28"), (1053, "17:33–17:51"), (1075, "17:55–18:33"),))
