#passage(("From my perspective, I think the struggle that is valued the most is the struggle with purpose. If the purpose of you going in that ring is for people to respect you, I think you've got a wrong perspective of how people should respect you. For example, I would respect a soldier who lost his arm, because of the reason he lost his arm. He was defending his country, defending his family, whatever he was doing. I'm just speaking in general, let's say for a Muslim (I'm really thinking from Islamic perspective here): a Muslim soldier who is fighting, like someone who's fighting (you can say right now in Palestine or any place), he's fighting for Truth. So if that person has something that is negative physically that happens to him, I respect him, because of the reasoning that he had behind that struggle.", "But this interaction that you had with this specific individual: he's got some mental issues, of course, to behave the way he behaved. The end result is not good! In the end, you can achieve all of these goals in a much better way. If something were to happen to you, and you were to actually die (and this is very possible, we're not talking about a very hypothetical thing here; the way he dealt with you, you could have died in that ring), now, your mission of what you said you want to do, seeking Truth or spreading the Truth or any of that, would have stopped! For what? It is not worth it, and it is not a smart decision to take. You've done it already, it's in the past, but I'm talking now from an analytical point of view: you could achieve all of these things that you talked about without specifically doing that thing with someone who's got some mental illness. How he behaves with a normal individual in comparison to someone else in the cage, these are two separate things. So there's something maybe to think about.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Muslim Lantern Gets Sneak∅ To Rethink His Views! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ocg_ABofNVU", ((