#passage(("You see in Islam a concept (some people have not heard it): if you find something on the floor, doesn't belong to you, so if it is of a value that the person is not going to come back look for it, then it's fine, you can take it. Let's say you 10 cents, 50 cents: no one is going to do that. So that's fine, you can keep. But if it's amount of money people going to come back for, let's say a phone or laptop or jewelry or [300, 400,] $500, something like this, people come back look for: a Muslim's responsibility, if he takes that money, that he has for a full year try to find the person who owns that money. One full year you have to do something called trying to find who is the owner of this property. Imagine one year! And you do effort; we're not just saying you go \"no, I didn't find anyone\". No, actually leaving your phone number or something like that: \"if anyone comes here, my phone, call me\". Then when someone calls: \"tell me the specifications, what did you lose, how much of the money\". What about one year and he didn't find it? You use the money. But what if he comes two years later? The [scholars] say you have to give him his money back. So [let's] say I have found $200; one year I tried to find, didn't find the person, I use the money. Two years later he comes, 10 years later he comes.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Eddie @TheDeenShowTV Interviews The Muslim Lantern | Masjid Dar Al-Qur'an | Chicago - USA”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sLfJyJGj_eg", ((3178, "52:58–54:21"),))

